{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The pen costs $1.25, derived by substituting N = P + 10 into N + P = 12.50, yielding 2P + 10 = 12.50 and P = 1.25.","counterconditions":["If 'costs $10 more than' were interpreted as a multiplicative relationship (e.g., N = 10 * P), then P would be approximately $1.136.","If the total cost were different, the price of the pen would change accordingly.","If 'costs $10 more' were interpreted as applying to the pen rather than the notebook (i.e., P = N + 10), no non-negative solution would exist."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Let P be the price of the pen and N be the price of the notebook. The problem states two constraints: (1) N + P = 12.50 and (2) N = P + 10. Substituting the second equation into the first yields (P + 10) + P = 12.50, which simplifies to 2P + 10 = 12.50, 2P = 2.50, and P = 1.25. This follows the covering principle of solving a system of two linear equations with two unknowns.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","c64876ec92c5fe85ec5b8e47cf9dd0ee140e0f8ade87c5feac133eed886a4069"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":1.0}],"simulation_proposals":[]}